{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_vrtx_011Cg2QmPtAuCLSVSen2pnR8", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I fixed the WAL file and recovered all 11 records into `/app/recovered.json`, sorted by id.\n\n**The problem:** every byte of `main.db-wal` had been XORed with `0x42`. I spotted it because the file's opening bytes are always the same in a WAL file, and here they came out as the expected value XORed with `0x42`. The file was also padded with `0x42` bytes, which are just zero bytes after that XOR. Reversing the XOR gave a valid WAL that SQLite accepted as it was, with no checksum errors.\n\n**Recovered data:**\n\n| id | name | value |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | apple | 150 |\n| 2 | banana | 250 |\n| 3 | cherry | 300 |\n| 4 | date | 400 |\n| 5 | elderberry | 500 |\n| 6 | fig | 600 |\n| 7 | grape | 700 |\n| 8 | honeydew | 800 |\n| 9 | kiwi | 900 |\n| 10 | lemon | 1000 |\n| 11 | mango | 1100 |\n\n**What's in `/app` now:** after I wrote the fixed WAL back, opening the database with SQLite merged the WAL changes into `main.db` and deleted the WAL file. So `/app/main.db` now holds all 11 records on its own, and there's no `main.db-wal` any more. Copies of the original encrypted WAL and the original database are saved in the container as `/tmp/main.db-wal` and `/tmp/main.db`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 2719, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16092, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 2719, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 501}, "input_transformations": [], "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "46001549-364d-4c5c-88d5-9c2c8f8e2437", "uuid": "18261127-e586-4ce0-897d-e9d23fccca56", "timestamp": "2026-10-14T16:02:13.171Z", "request_id": "req_vrtx_011Cg2QmPqwKn9BihMeszLKd"}
